    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="0797" n="783" sameAs="frus:frus1943v06/pg783"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">833.51/1012: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Uruguay</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-01-18T17:00:00-02:30">January 18, 1943—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>87. According to an Associated Press despatch, Guani<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d647-f1"/> said yesterday in a press interview in Buenos Aires that
                    from his talks in Washington there will probably result a 100 million peso loan
                    for Public Works. In a press interview given in Montevideo the previous day he
                    talked of a 5-year Public Works plan and a large loan without mentioning any
                    specific amount.</p>
                <p>I have, of course, given Guani no encouragement to expect a large loan.
                    Furthermore, in our various conversations I had pointed out consistently that it
                    would be unfortunate if people here were allowed to expect too much from his
                    visit and in particular if they gained the advance impression that a sizeable
                    loan would be sought or abounding.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d647-f1" n="45" xml:id="d647-f1-content">
                <p>Alberto Guani, Uruguayan Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
